Land titles have characteristics that are inextricably tied to their ownership history and evolve through time. The document chain ensures that land ownership is authentic, which is consistent with the core idea of blockchain. Every new block in the blockchain is linked to the one before it, forming an unbreakable chain that can be traced back to the root node. Transfers of land ownership are compatible with this functionality. Once approved by the peer-to-peer blockchain community, a new block becomes immutable and nearly impossible to change. This blockchain feature is useful in preventing document fraud by influential people. Moreover, every node in the blockchain network maintains a complete copy of the entire ledger, ensuring transparency. Although other DLTs such as Tangle and Hashgraph exist, they have not yet been tested in this domain, unlike blockchain, which has been proposed for similar applications. Since every country has its own unique cultural and historical model of land title management, it is crucial to find an appropriate solution for the maintenance of land ROR in India using modern technology such as blockchain. The current system suffers from a major issue of fragmented information scattered across multiple government offices that is poorly synchronized, and corrupt individuals can conspire to manipulate legal documents. Thus, a centralized system alone would not suffice to tackle the various land titling frauds. However, DLT, such as blockchain, can offer valuable solutions to this problem. DLT is a database model that connects multiple nodes in a peer-to-peer network, with each node storing a complete copy of the ledger. Any change made to the ledger is subject to approval by a majority (e.g., PoW, PoS) of the connected nodes, making it highly resistant to falsification by any single node or group of nodes. 

Since its creation by Satoshi Nakamoto in 2008, blockchain has been transforming the technology landscape. Although its early version, Blockchain 1.0, was primarily used for cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin, newer versions of Blockchain, including 2.0, 3.0, and 4.0, have presented immense opportunities. over the past decade. In these newer models, smart contracts play a vital role, acting as small scripts that ensure that data-related policies are verified before a new block is added to the blockchain ledger through a successful mining operation. Given that millions of smart contracts are deployed in public blockchain ecosystems, it is crucial to focus on their security vulnerabilities. Additionally, the growing adoption of 5G technologies present opportunities for increased incorporation of IoT and AI in the blockchain domain. Smart contracts in the age of 5G, IoT, and AI can provide further improvements to the land titling model in India. 

The land management policy in India currently relies on a paper-based system, which is both traditional and 

cumbersome. This system suffers from a lack of synchronization between different departments, leading to various types of fraudulent activities. Moreover, transferring the Record of Rights can take several months, and identifying court cases or bank leases during the registration of Ownership transfers are also challenging. To overcome these issues, we propose incorporating blockchain technology, which offers inherent properties such as transparency, immutable record management, easier synchronization, and wider accessibility. 

The digitization process in India is something that has started very recently. A lot of information is still on paper, which is going through the process of digitization. One such source of information is the land record registry. The whole ownership of the land record registry is with the government, and various verification tasks rely on the government. Moreover, a single data point is prone to attacks and hacks. People can also tamper with information due to corruption. This tampering affects the genuine owners of the land, who might be uneducated and unaware. Blockchain has been a new evolution in technology, allowing total control for the user and providing state-of-the-art encryption capabilities. Though blockchain has been used to store land records and other records as well, the government still has major control, and the verification process is still time-consuming. The only addition in this layer is the bank, which still makes the process time-consuming. We need another layer, a new framework, at the ground level that makes the verification process faster and more secure and, at the same time, gives control to the user (owner of the land). 

Technology is ever-evolving, which means that our current frameworks need to keep up and adapt to the changes. The current land registry system needs a change from its single data source. Blockchain is a new concept that has gained a lot of attention over the last few years. The need for a new registry system, and the features of blockchain go hand in hand. The main motivation is to provide the concept of decentralization at the root level in terms of the land system of India and make a faster and more efficient verification system. A faster and more secure verification system will further lead to many applications, such as land bidding, land acquisition, and the construction of new properties for further development. 

The main objectives of this paper are the following: 

- Developing a blockchain-based framework for the land verification system. 

- Developing an efficient and automated process of storing previous hard copy land records into the network using deep learning-based information extraction. 

- Implementing attribute-based fog devices over a blockchain network. 

- Adding a ground-level verification system on top of the current planned layer by the government. 

