The module creates a plane object for the positioning of each word after applying text classification, similar to Module 1.1. Each text object is called a text embedding, which is passed over the plane and assigned over coordinates called position embeddings (PE). The embeddings are decided by the deep convolutional network called Faster R- CNN. Since each embedding is treated as an object, the faster R-CNN gives efficient and accurate results. 

Once information is extracted from the document, a Region of Interest (ROI) pooling operation takes place, similar to how modern-day cameras detect people from images or live video streams. The model considers each object as a whole, provides weightage to it, and then positions each field on the plane object created through position embeddings. The position embeddings are only two coordinates at a time, though multiple concurrent embedding processes can take place based on the amount of computing power and memory available. 

There are various models that use BERT to perform various NLP tasks, but typically these models only utilize text information from any input. So BERT alone cannot be used for our use case because we are dealing with hardcopy documents that can be in any language other than English and can vary from region to region. This kind of document contains a huge amount of information that cannot be encoded and processed to pass through a BERT-trained model. We require LayoutLM’s knowledge of different land document layouts and how information is arranged in them in order to resolve issues. 

Moreover, LayoutLM provides various extensions and easy-to-use frameworks that can be used based on the requirements of the user. Various versions of LayoutLM have since been rolled out, which tackle various extraction processes and provide high efficiency and accuracy. 

Once the relevant attributes have been identified, each entry is converted to its md5 hash (a cryptographic hash function that generates a fixed-size output) because each object in the array subsequently becomes too large to process and transfer over the network. These hashes are then passed through a converter, which turns them into a Merkle hash tree. The Merkle hash tree is a powerful data structure that enables efficient and secure verification of the integrity of large data sets. The root hash of the Merkle tree is then sent to Module 3, which utilizes this information to further analyze and interpret the data. By utilizing the root hash, Module 3 can efficiently verify the integrity of the data and ensure that it has not been tampered with in any way. 

The Merkle Hash Tree is a data structure based on the tree data structure, which is used in cryptography. Its most significant use is in the blockchain. Prominent blockchains such as Bitcoin and Ethereum use forms of the Merkle Hash Tree in their workings and various components. The Merkle Hash Tree works by hashing the child nodes and then adding them to create a respective parent node. For example, in the above diagram, L1, L2, L3, and L4 are the data points that need to be processed. These data points are passed as child nodes and are hashed. Then a hash of L1 and a hash of L2 are added and stored in Hash 0 in the diagram. A similar thing is done for Hash 1. Then the top hash is created by adding hash 0 and hash 1, then hashing the result of the addition. The top hash is then created, which stores the whole information in a single place. The top hash is also called the root hash. The Merkle Hash Tree provides easy tracking of each data point, making it convenient and efficient when dealing with large files. 

